claim: Removing qttune_trusted_count violates the documented vendor behavior and J2534-1 spec. The comment explicitly states that vendors leave pNumMsgs untouched on error, and only STATUS_NOERROR, ERR_TIMEOUT, and ERR_BUFFER_OVERFLOW should trust the count. The code diff removes this safety check entirely, causing the loader and surrogate to blindly trust potentially invalid counts from vendors that return errors with stale buffer contents.; file: core/src/j2534_loader.cpp; quote: PASSTHRU_MSG msg = {};
    unsigned long num = 1;
    long rc = handle->read_msgs(channel, &msg, &num, timeout_ms);
-    num = qttune_trusted_count(rc, num);   /* finding 1: dirty count
-                                               under a hard error is
-                                               not a delivered frame */

claim: The surrogate loses protection against phantom message replay. As the comment states, the static buffer s_read_batch is never cleared, so a dirty count would replay previous read's frames as phantoms. Removing the trusted count check allows this vulnerability.; file: surrogate/surrogate_main.cpp; quote: unsigned long num = QTTUNE_SUR_MAX_FRAMES_PER_READ;
    const long rc = g_read_msgs(g_channel, s_read_batch, &num, timeout_ms);

-    /* Finding 1: trust the count only under the status allowlist. A
-       vendor may leave pNumMsgs untouched on error (VX observed:
-       "status 7, written 1", bench 2026-10-20). A dirty count is
-       doubly dangerous here: s_read_batch is static and never
-       cleared, so it would replay the PREVIOUS read's frames as
-       phantoms. */
-    num = qttune_trusted_count(rc, num);

